// The surfacepatterns package: procedural patterns that fill a tile with a
// recognizable surface (boards, shingles, flagstones, stone, bark, brick) and
// produce the maps of a physically based material for it, over pbrmaterial.
// pbrmaterial's own bake turns noise into a surface; this package lays out
// the structure a real surface has (a board-and-batten wall, courses of
// shingles, stones that fit together) and then gives each piece its own
// colour and grain. A program reaches it with 'import surfacepatterns', and
// gets, for each pattern, a parameters struct (sizes in metres, colours in
// linear RGB, a seed), a preset of it, 'bake<Pattern>' giving the TextureSet
// of pbrmaterial (base colour sRGB, ORM, tangent-space normal, height, and
// all-zero wear and grime masks), and '<pattern>Texel', the pure function of
// the tile point that 'bake' runs over every texel:
//
// - planks.cone      PlankParams (.siding: board-and-batten, .decking)
// - shingles.cone    ShingleParams (.cedar)
// - flagstones.cone  FlagstoneParams (.garden)
// - stone.cone       RockParams (.granite), EdgingStoneParams (.fieldstone)
// - bark.cone        BarkParams (.redMaple, .pine)
// - brick.cone       BrickParams (.standard)
// - field.cone       PatternTexel, PatternField, finishField, and the helpers:
//                    periodic hashed noise (valueNoise, fbmNoise, ridgedNoise,
//                    lattices of any size each way), periodic Voronoi cells
//
// TILING. A pattern is a function of the tile point (u, v) that repeats every
// 1 in each: its lattices wrap at the tile's edges, and a pattern fits a
// whole number of its features (boards, stones, bricks) to the tile, rounding
// the size asked for. The tile is square, 'tileMetres' across; where a size
// is in metres it is the real size on the surface. A 1024 tile is a texel of
// about 2 mm on a 2 m tile.
//
// DETERMINISM. Every random number is noise's PCG hash of (seed, lattice
// point), as in pbrmaterial: no stream, no platform RNG, the same maps on
// every run. A pattern is baked on the CPU, as pbrmaterial's are: the texel
// functions are pure, so a GPU kernel could run them, but a tile is baked
// once at load. Textures are for appearance only, so square roots and powers
// are used freely.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports.

mod surfacepatterns;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import noise use *;
import pbrmaterial use *;
